#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163294 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two random variables with covariance $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ and standard deviations $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$.\n\nThe **correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$,** also known as the **Pearson correlation coefficient**, is denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\") and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X]\\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The correlation coefficient satisfies\n$-1\\leq \\rho \\leq 1$.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then\n$\\rho = 0$.\nThe reverse implication does not always hold. In other words, $\\rho=0$ does *not* imply that $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n- Unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ is a *dimensionless* quantity. This means we'll get the same answer regardless of the units used to measure $X$ and $Y$.\n- Also unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ tells the strength of the correlation between $X$ and $Y$. We typically have the following rule of thumb:If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data generated by our two random variables closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data generated by our random variables follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** No (linear) correlation exists between the data points generated by the random variables.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)\n\nLet's get some practice computing the correlation coefficient." :tutorial/id #uuid "96cc345f-656a-5faf-a2cf-47946504320d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5638 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
